--- title The infix operators
--- about Every operator is a second way to reach a prefix native, so
--- about what each one pins is which native it reaches. Two of the nine
--- about here are not the twin the spelling suggests, and both were
--- about settled by comparing bodies in R3 3.22.1 rather than guessed.

--- id operators/not-equal-when-they-differ
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 != 2
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/not-equal-when-they-do-not
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 != 1
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/strictly-not-equal-minds-the-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note One and one-point-zero are the same magnitude and different
--- note datatypes, which is the whole difference from !=.
--- code
1 !== 1.0
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/strictly-not-equal-on-two-of-a-kind
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 !== 1
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-is-never-negative
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note %% is MOD, not MODULO and not REMAINDER. Reading it as
--- note REMAINDER gives -1 here, which is the answer you get by
--- note assuming rather than asking.
--- code
-7 %% 3
--- result
2

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-with-a-negative-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
7 %% -3
--- result
1

--- id operators/bitwise-and
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
5 & 3
--- result
1

--- id operators/bitwise-or
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 | 2
--- result
3

--- id operators/raising-to-a-power-gives-a-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Even when both sides are whole and the answer is whole.
--- code
2 ** 10
--- result
1024.0

--- id operators/shifting-left
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 << 4
--- result
16

--- id operators/shifting-right
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
16 >> 2
--- result
4

--- id operators/identity-holds-for-one-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note =? is SAME?, not EQUAL?. Two integers of equal magnitude are
--- note one value, so this is true.
--- code
1 =? 1
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/identity-fails-for-two-equal-strings
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And this is why the distinction matters: these two strings are
--- note equal and are not the same one.
--- code
"a" =? "a"
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/two-percents-is-a-word-not-a-file
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The boundary that matters for reading %% at all: % is the file
--- note sigil, so a reader that takes %% as a file leaves the operator
--- note unreachable and the expression around it quietly wrong.
--- code
type? first [%%]
--- result
#(word!)

--- id operators/one-percent-alone-is-a-word-too
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A file needs a name after the sigil. Nothing after it is not an
--- note empty file, it is the word.
--- code
type? first [%]
--- result
#(word!)

--- id operators/a-percent-with-a-name-is-a-file
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note One character past the boundary, and it reads the other way.
--- code
type? first [%a]
--- result
#(file!)

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-of-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
0 %% 3
--- result
0

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-just-below-the-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
-1 %% 3
--- result
2

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-at-the-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
-3 %% 3
--- result
0

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-by-zero-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
7 %% 0
--- error math zero-divide

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-with-a-fractional-dividend
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
7.5 %% 2
--- result
1.5

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-with-a-fractional-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And the answer is a whole number rather than 2.0, which is not
--- note what mixing a decimal in usually does.
--- code
7 %% 2.5
--- result
2

--- id operators/shifting-left-by-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
1 << 0
--- result
1

--- id operators/shifting-left-into-the-sign-bit
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note One short of the width. The answer is negative, because the bit
--- note that moved into place is the sign.
--- code
1 << 63
--- result
-9223372036854775808

--- id operators/shifting-left-by-the-whole-width
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Not zero. The count wraps, so shifting by 64 is shifting by 0.
--- code
1 << 64
--- result
1

--- id operators/shifting-left-by-a-negative-count
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Also not what wrapping would give: a plain wrap sends -1 to 63
--- note and the answer to the sign bit. R3 answers 1.
--- code
1 << -1
--- result
1

--- id operators/shifting-right-by-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
8 >> 0
--- result
8

--- id operators/shifting-right-by-the-whole-width
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
8 >> 64
--- result
8

--- id operators/shifting-a-negative-right-keeps-the-sign
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
-8 >> 1
--- result
-4

--- id operators/anything-to-the-power-of-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
2 ** 0
--- result
1.0

--- id operators/a-negative-power-is-a-reciprocal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
2 ** -1
--- result
0.5

--- id operators/zero-to-the-power-of-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
0 ** 0
--- result
1.0

--- id operators/bitwise-and-with-all-bits-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
-1 & 5
--- result
5

--- id operators/bitwise-or-with-all-bits-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
-1 | 5
--- result
-1

--- id operators/identity-across-datatypes
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Same magnitude, different datatype, so not the same value.
--- code
1 =? 1.0
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/identity-of-a-shared-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Two names for one block. This is what =? is for.
--- code
a: [1]
b: a
a =? b
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/identity-of-a-copied-series
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Equal contents, two blocks. EQUAL? would say true here.
--- code
a: [1]
b: copy [1]
a =? b
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/identity-of-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
none =? none
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/not-equal-folds-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
"a" != "A"
--- result
#(false)

--- id operators/strictly-not-equal-minds-case
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the pair that shows != and !== are not the same
--- note question asked twice.
--- code
"a" !== "A"
--- result
#(true)

--- id operators/shifting-left-by-a-large-count-wraps
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note 65 wraps to 1, so the count is taken modulo the width.
--- code
1 << 65
--- result
2

--- id operators/shifting-right-by-a-negative-count
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Negative counts do nothing in either direction, which is not
--- note what wrapping the count would give.
--- code
8 >> -1
--- result
8

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-takes-the-dividends-datatype
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note 8 mod 2.5 is 0.5, and the answer is 0. The dividend is a whole
--- note number, so the answer is one too, and the fraction is dropped
--- note rather than carried.
--- code
8 %% 2.5
--- result
0

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-with-a-decimal-dividend-stays-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The same division the other way round. Only the dividend
--- note decides.
--- code
7.0 %% 2.5
--- result
2.0

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-of-a-character
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note MOD takes characters, times and money as well as numbers, and
--- note the dividend's datatype decides the answer's throughout. A
--- note character in gives a character out.
--- code
#"a" %% 3
--- result
#"^A"

--- id operators/a-character-as-the-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The other way round: the character is read as its code point
--- note and the answer is a whole number.
--- code
1000 %% #"a"
--- result
30

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-of-a-time
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
10:0 %% 3:0
--- result
1:00

--- id operators/euclidean-modulo-of-money
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
$10 %% 3
--- result
$1
